META ARGUMENTS:-
PARALLELISM: By default terraform follows parallelism.
it will execute all the resource at a time.
By default parallelism limit is 10.
Terraform can create maximum 10 resource at a time.
Terraform apply -auto-approve -parallelism=1
Note:-IT WILL APPLICABLE FOR BOTH APPLY & DESTROY.

DEPENDS ON:One resource creation will be depending on other
this is called explicit dependency.

e.g

provider "aws" {
    region= "eu-north-1"
}

resource "aws_instance" "one" {
    ami= "ami-046d18c147c36bef1"
    instance_type= "t3.mircro"
    tags={
        Name= "test-server"
    }
}

resource "aws_s3_bucket" "two" {
    bucket = "terraformbucket123"
    depends_on = [aws_instance.one]
}

2.COUNT : used to create similar objects.

provider "aws" {
  region = "eu-north-1"
}

resource "aws_instance" "one" {
  count         = 3
  ami           = "ami-046d18c147c36bef1"
  instance_type = "t3.micro"
  tags = {
    Name = "dev-server-${count.index+1}"
  }
}

NOTE:-It won't create resource with diferent config.

3.FOR-EACH-->It is a loop used to create resource
we can pass same code with different configuration.It will create resource with less code.

provider "aws" {
    region= "eu-north-1"
}

resource "aws_instance" "one" {
    for_each=toset(["dev-server","test-server","prod-server"])
    ami="ami-0a716d3f3b16d290c"
    instance_type="t3.micro"
    tags={
        Name="${each.key}
    }
}

4.Life Cycle:-

PREVENT DESTROY:- It is used to prevent the resource to not be deleted.

provider "aws" {
    region:"eu-north-1"
}

resource "aws_instance" "one" {
  ami           = "ami-046d18c147c36bef1"
  instance_type = "t2.micro"
  tags = {
    Name = "raham-server"
  }
  lifecycle {
    prevent_destroy = true
  }
}

NOTE: CHECK WITH TERRAFORM APPLY

IGNORE CHANGES:
when user modify anything on current state manually changes will be ignored.

provider "aws" {
  region = "us-east-1"
}

resource "aws_instance" "one" {
  ami           = "ami-046d18c147c36bef1"
  instance_type = "t2.micro"
  tags = {
    Name = "raham-server"
  }
  lifecycle {
    ignore_changes = all
  }
}

NOTE: MODIFY NAME MANUALLY AND CHECK WITH TERRAFORM APPLY

CREATE BEFORE DESTROY:
by default when we modify some properties terraform will first destroy old server and create new server later.

if we follow create before destroy lifecycle the new resource will create first
and later old resource is going to be destroyed.

provider "aws" {
  region = "us-east-1"
}

resource "aws_instance" "one" {
  ami           = "ami-0208b77a23d891325"
  instance_type = "t2.micro"
  tags = {
    Name = "raham-server"
  }
  lifecycle {
    create_before_destroy = true
  }
}

TERRAFORM IMPORT:-
when we create resource manually terraform won't track that resource.
import command can used to import the resource which is created manually.
can only import one resource at a time(FROM CLI)
it can import both code to config file and state file.
FOR STATE FILe: terraform import aws_instance.one(not preferable)

FIRST CREATE A MANUAL SERVER.

cat main.tf

provider "aws" {
  region = "us-east-1"
}

import {
  to = aws_instance.one
  id = "i-0c23bdc1b7b73d61c"
}

FOR STATEFILE & CODE: 
terraform plan -generate-config-out=ec2.tf
NOTE: DELETE LINE 20 AND 21 FROM ec2.tf
terraform apply -auto-approve
terraform state list
terraform destroy -auto-approve

